{"subjectId":"python","id":"itertools-module","title":"itertools Deep Dive","group":"Advanced Python","order":41,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>itertools</code> is a toolkit of fast, memory-efficient iterator building blocks written in C. Combine them to express loops as pipelines.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-infinity\"></i>Infinite iterators</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from itertools import count, cycle, repeat, islice\n\nprint(list(islice(count(10, 5), 4)))          # [10, 15, 20, 25]\nprint(list(islice(cycle(\"AB\"), 5)))           # ['A', 'B', 'A', 'B', 'A']\nprint(list(repeat(\"x\", 3)))                   # ['x', 'x', 'x']\nprint(list(map(pow, [2, 3, 4], repeat(2))))   # [4, 9, 16]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>count()</code> and <code>cycle()</code> never stop — always bound them with <code>islice</code>, <code>zip</code>, <code>takewhile</code> or a <code>break</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>Slicing &amp; filtering</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from itertools import islice, takewhile, dropwhile, filterfalse, compress\n\nnums = [1, 4, 6, 3, 8, 2]\nprint(list(islice(nums, 1, 5, 2)))                  # [4, 3]\nprint(list(takewhile(lambda n: n &lt; 5, nums)))       # [1, 4]\nprint(list(dropwhile(lambda n: n &lt; 5, nums)))       # [6, 3, 8, 2]\nprint(list(filterfalse(lambda n: n % 2, nums)))     # [4, 6, 8, 2]\nprint(list(compress(\"abcdef\", [1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1]))) # ['a', 'c', 'f']</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-join\"></i>Combining &amp; reshaping</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from itertools import chain, zip_longest, pairwise, batched, starmap, accumulate\nimport operator\n\nprint(list(chain.from_iterable([[1, 2], [3], []])))       # [1, 2, 3]\nprint(list(zip_longest(\"abc\", [1], fillvalue=0)))          # [('a', 1), ('b', 0), ('c', 0)]\nprint(list(pairwise([1, 4, 9, 16])))                       # [(1, 4), (4, 9), (9, 16)]\nprint(list(batched(range(7), 3)))                          # [(0, 1, 2), (3, 4, 5), (6,)]\nprint(list(starmap(operator.mul, [(2, 3), (4, 5)])))       # [6, 20]\nprint(list(accumulate([3, 1, 4, 1, 5], max)))              # [3, 3, 4, 4, 5]\nprint(list(accumulate([1, 2, 3], initial=100)))            # [100, 101, 103, 106]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Since</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>chain.from_iterable(xss)</code></td><td>—</td><td>Flatten one level lazily</td></tr><tr><td><code>zip_longest(a, b, fillvalue=…)</code></td><td>—</td><td>Zip without truncating to the shortest</td></tr><tr><td><code>pairwise(it)</code></td><td>3.10</td><td>Consecutive pairs — differences, gaps, edges of a path</td></tr><tr><td><code>batched(it, n)</code></td><td>3.12</td><td>Fixed-size chunks (<code>strict=True</code> in 3.13 demands full batches)</td></tr><tr><td><code>accumulate(it, func)</code></td><td>—</td><td>Running totals / max / products (prefix sums)</td></tr><tr><td><code>starmap(f, pairs)</code></td><td>—</td><td><code>f(*args)</code> for each tuple of args</td></tr><tr><td><code>tee(it, n)</code></td><td>—</td><td>Split one iterator into <code>n</code> independent ones (buffers items)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-atom-2\"></i>Combinatorics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from itertools import product, permutations, combinations, combinations_with_replacement\nfrom math import comb, perm\n\nprint(list(product(\"ab\", repeat=2)))               # [('a', 'a'), ('a', 'b'), ('b', 'a'), ('b', 'b')]\nprint(list(permutations(\"abc\", 2)))                # [('a', 'b'), ('a', 'c'), ('b', 'a'), ('b', 'c'), ('c', 'a'), ('c', 'b')]\nprint(list(combinations([1, 2, 3], 2)))            # [(1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 3)]\nprint(list(combinations_with_replacement(\"ab\", 2)))  # [('a', 'a'), ('a', 'b'), ('b', 'b')]\nprint(len(list(permutations(range(5), 3))), perm(5, 3), comb(5, 3))   # 60 60 10</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Order matters?</th><th>Repeats?</th><th>Count for n items, size r</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>product(xs, repeat=r)</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>nʳ</td></tr><tr><td><code>permutations(xs, r)</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>n! / (n−r)!</td></tr><tr><td><code>combinations(xs, r)</code></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>n! / (r!(n−r)!)</td></tr><tr><td><code>combinations_with_replacement(xs, r)</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>(n+r−1)! / (r!(n−1)!)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>groupby — done right</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from itertools import groupby\nfrom operator import itemgetter\n\nsales = [(\"fruit\", \"apple\"), (\"veg\", \"kale\"), (\"fruit\", \"kiwi\"), (\"veg\", \"leek\")]\nprint([k for k, _ in groupby(sales, key=itemgetter(0))])   # ['fruit', 'veg', 'fruit', 'veg']\n\nsales.sort(key=itemgetter(0))                               # sort by the SAME key first\ngrouped = {k: [name for _, name in g] for k, g in groupby(sales, key=itemgetter(0))}\nprint(grouped)                                              # {'fruit': ['apple', 'kiwi'], 'veg': ['kale', 'leek']}\n\n# Run-length encoding: the classic groupby interview question\nprint(\"\".join(f\"{ch}{len(list(g))}\" for ch, g in groupby(\"aaabccdd\")))   # a3b1c2d2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Each group is an iterator tied to the parent <code>groupby</code>. Advancing to the next group invalidates the previous one — consume (e.g. <code>list(g)</code>) before moving on.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chef-hat\"></i>Recipes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from itertools import islice, chain, cycle\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef sliding_window(it, n):\n    it = iter(it)\n    window = deque(islice(it, n - 1), maxlen=n)\n    for x in it:\n        window.append(x)\n        yield tuple(window)\n\ndef unique_everseen(it):\n    seen = set()\n    for x in it:\n        if x not in seen:\n            seen.add(x)\n            yield x\n\ndef roundrobin(*its):\n    iterators = cycle(iter(i) for i in its)\n    pending = len(its)\n    while pending:\n        try:\n            yield next(next(iterators))\n        except StopIteration:\n            pending -= 1\n            iterators = cycle(list(islice(iterators, pending)))\n\nprint(list(sliding_window([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 3)))   # [(1, 2, 3), (2, 3, 4), (3, 4, 5)]\nprint(list(unique_everseen(\"abracadabra\")))       # ['a', 'b', 'r', 'c', 'd']\nprint(list(roundrobin(\"ABC\", \"D\", \"EF\")))         # ['A', 'D', 'E', 'B', 'F', 'C']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The official docs end with an <em>Itertools Recipes</em> section; the third-party <code>more-itertools</code> package ships all of them (and many more) ready to import.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you generate all subsets of a list?</summary><div><p><code>chain.from_iterable(combinations(xs, r) for r in range(len(xs) + 1))</code> — 2ⁿ subsets, produced lazily. Alternatively, loop a bitmask from <code>0</code> to <code>2**n - 1</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does groupby return the same key more than once?</summary><div><p>It only groups <strong>consecutive</strong> items with equal keys, like the Unix <code>uniq</code> command. Sort by the same key first, or use a <code>defaultdict(list)</code> if order doesn't matter.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the benefit of itertools over writing loops?</summary><div><p>They are implemented in C and lazy: less memory (items are produced on demand), often faster, composable into pipelines, and they express intent (<code>pairwise</code>, <code>batched</code>) instead of index arithmetic.</p></div></details></section>"}